# Shell used on Linux CI; CRLF breaks bash -e / shebang parsing on checkout.
*.sh text eol=lf
.githooks/* text eol=lf

# KAT vectors are reference data pinned by SHA-256 in kats-manifest.toml and checked
# by scripts/ci_guard_kat_provenance.py. Git must not rewrite their bytes per-platform:
# with `core.autocrlf=true` a Windows clone holds CRLF while the blobs and CI hold LF,
# so the same commit hashes two different ways and the provenance check fires on every
# file for a reason unrelated to drift. `-text` disables all conversion.
lib-q-hqc/kats/** -text
*.rsp -text
*.req -text

# The NIST LWC fixtures are `.txt`, so none of the patterns above reached them and they were
# checked out CRLF on Windows while the blob stayed LF. Demonstrated on
# LWC_AEAD_KAT_256_128_saturninshortv2.txt: stored blob sha256 9936c724... which is exactly the
# `upstream_sha256` in kats-manifest.toml, versus 9e9cc929... for the same file in the worktree.
# The provenance guard normalises line endings so it passed either way, but a plain `sha256sum` on
# a fresh clone contradicted the manifest -- which is precisely the kind of unexplained mismatch
# that teaches people to distrust the check.
lib-q-saturnin/tests/fixtures/** -text
lib-q-romulus/tests/fixtures/** -text
lib-q-mayo/tests/kats/** -text
